schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Planning Web3 Events for Your Local Node
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A practitioner playbook for running local-node events without burning out
    organizers. Core moves: pick one job per event (educational, social,
    working, or celebration — never all four at once); tie web3 tooling to a
    concrete local question rather than leading with tokenomics; follow a
    five-phase repeatable plan (define the job, set logistics, promote,
    run the day, follow up within 24 hours); budget for the tier you can
    repeat monthly rather than a heroic one-off; and rotate hosting/roles so
    no single person becomes the node. Includes a current (mid-2026) survey of
    event tooling costs and status changes (Luma, Meetup, Eventbrite, Gather,
    POAP, Galxe OAT).
  audience: local node stewards / community organizers
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Attendance-badge collections (POAP/Galxe OAT) are framed as a nice
      touch, but the article also notes they "quietly become your node's
      on-chain history of who showed up," which "can later inform who gets a
      vote, a role, or a share of a grant" — a governance-relevant use the
      article flags but does not fully work through.
  related_concepts:
    - one-job-per-event
  related_resources:
    - luma
    - galxe-oat
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/planning-web3-events.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
